    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i'm a bit worried about the joist spacing though. if the dead load from the cedar itself is higher than i thought, or if there's some unexpected stress on the connections, i don't want the whole thing to fail or have structural issues. what are some of the common ways a deck structure ends up failing like that?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Common deck failures often stem from undersized posts leading to post-bottom wedge split or rutting, loosening bolts at heel plates allowing the post to pull out, or sheathing shear where joists fail at the tread connection. When live or dead loads exceed design limits due to poor spacing or moisture damage, these weak points become critical failure sites. To prevent this, always verify your lumber grade and ensure the foundation is a solid concrete pad capable of handling the combined weight without shifting.",
      "source": "target",
